WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Differences between a DFSS and PFSS Project

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.733
Welcome to this session on Design for Six Sigma.

3
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:13.999
Or what we call DFSS Design for Six Sigma.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.833
This is a very, very powerful concept, and normally follows Processing for Six Sigma.

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:27.133
Remember the aim of Processing for Six Sigma, PFSS.

6
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:32.666
Here's a scale of measure.

7
00:00:32.666 --> 00:00:38.232
Here is a performance distribution, say of some CTQ.

8
00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:44.033
And, let's suppose that, here's the target.

9
00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:48.333
Thus, the average of this distribution,

10
00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:52.399
is right there. That's the center of the capability that you have.

11
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.133
Notice it’s separated from the target.

12
00:00:56.133 --> 00:01:01.766
There's the target. And it's off location.

13
00:01:01.766 --> 00:01:08.766
Well, what's our goal of PFSS? Our goal is to take that distribution and put it on center.

14
00:01:08.766 --> 00:01:13.266
And narrow the variation.

15
00:01:13.266 --> 00:01:19.999
See, that's our goal. We’re going to zoom in there and really start to focus on that.

16
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.566
Processing for Six Sigma is about

17
00:01:24.566 --> 00:01:29.699
putting the center of that distribution on the target

18
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.233
and concurrently narrowing the spread. The variation.

19
00:01:34.233 --> 00:01:39.566
Cause remember, that variation is the enemy. It constitutes uncertainty.

20
00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:42.599
And we want to remove uncertainty.

21
00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:48.033
We want to make our processes operate as certain as possible.

22
00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:52.999
Now this is all fine and well but it's relative to something.

23
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:00.000
What is that goal relative to? It’s relative to this target.

24
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.200
Who sets that target? Recall here, if I look at this,

25
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.266
I can view this as an is condition.

26
00:02:09.266 --> 00:02:13.132
Is. See, there's the average. That's the is.

27
00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:18.999
And the target here is the ought. And the difference is a delta.

28
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.966
Or gap, as we call it.

29
00:02:21.966 --> 00:02:24.999
Now who sets this?

30
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:30.800
Who is responsible out there, to determine what ought to be?

31
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.666
Obviously, we know how to determine is, we do that through PFSS.

32
00:02:36.666 --> 00:02:40.666
The ought is done through DFSS.

33
00:02:40.666 --> 00:02:46.666
And that's what we’re going to explore as we talk about Design for Six Sigma.

34
00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:53.499
How to set the expectations, such as the upper spec limit, the target,

35
00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.500
and the lower specification limits?

36
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:01.500
How do we set the standards? The performance expectation?

37
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:07.100
How do we establish the window of operation for our CTQ’s?

38
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:13.866
That's what Design for Six Sigma is about. Setting those standards and expectations.

39
00:03:13.866 --> 00:03:18.866
Thank you for choosing SSMI.

